WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.533 --> 00:00:08.893
In the first film's model, defenses must remove more than half the bacteria every

2
00:00:08.893 --> 00:00:14.313
doubling time. But who does the removing: cells, or something dissolved in the

3
00:00:14.313 --> 00:00:15.313
blood?

4
00:00:15.617 --> 00:00:20.917
In the 1880s and 1890s, immunologists argued over exactly this.

5
00:00:22.057 --> 00:00:24.857
Elie Metchnikoff, a zoologist, championed the cells.

6
00:00:26.117 --> 00:00:28.017
He chose an animal he could see through.

7
00:00:30.017 --> 00:00:33.157
The water flea Daphnia is small and transparent.

8
00:00:34.477 --> 00:00:38.537
Some fleas swallow the needle-shaped spores of a budding yeast with their food.

9
00:00:39.797 --> 00:00:42.437
The spores pierce the gut wall into the body cavity.

10
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.160
Suppose the flea's blood cells were passive, or even carried the infection.

11
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.320
What would you expect them to do with the spores?

12
00:00:57.967 --> 00:01:01.207
Passive cells should ignore the spores, or spread them.

13
00:01:02.347 --> 00:01:07.167
Instead, Metchnikoff saw mobile cells crowd onto the spores, surround them, and

14
00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:08.167
engulf them.

15
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.720
The outcome tracked the flea's fate.

16
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.980
Where the cells destroyed the spores, the infection stopped.

17
00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.680
Where spores escaped and grew into budding yeast, the flea died.

18
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:26.110
His 1884 paper made the eating cell, the phagocyte, an active defender.

19
00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:29.510
But it did not show that the cells work alone.

20
00:01:30.583 --> 00:01:36.983
In Paris in the 1890s, Jules Bordet studied serum, the fluid that remains once blood

21
00:01:36.983 --> 00:01:43.203
clots. Serum from an animal immunized against a cholera bacterium killed those

22
00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:44.203
bacteria.

23
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:49.273
Heated to about 55 °C, the immune serum lost its power.

24
00:01:50.573 --> 00:01:55.413
Fresh serum from an animal never immunized could not kill the bacteria on its own

25
00:01:55.413 --> 00:01:56.413
either.

26
00:01:56.733 --> 00:02:00.993
Then Bordet mixed the heated immune serum with the fresh normal serum.

27
00:02:02.113 --> 00:02:03.433
The mixture killed again.

28
00:02:04.573 --> 00:02:06.433
So the killing needs two separate parts.

29
00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:11.007
One part survives heat and is raised by immunization.

30
00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:13.467
We now call it antibody.

31
00:02:14.747 --> 00:02:18.567
The other is destroyed by heat and is present in all fresh serum.

32
00:02:19.747 --> 00:02:20.747
We call it complement.

33
00:02:21.867 --> 00:02:23.267
So which side was right?

34
00:02:24.507 --> 00:02:26.187
Watch a bacterium meet complement.

35
00:02:27.487 --> 00:02:31.127
Small green tags, a piece called C3b, coat its surface.

36
00:02:32.507 --> 00:02:35.147
A neutrophil grabs the coated bacterium quickly.

37
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:38.850
Coated microbes are eaten far more readily.

38
00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:42.530
In the first film's race, that raises k.

39
00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:46.770
The cells eat, and the proteins make the eating faster.

40
00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:53.540
In 1908, Metchnikoff shared the Nobel Prize with Paul Ehrlich, who stressed the

41
00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:54.600
defenses in serum.

42
00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:58.200
Each side had described part of one system.
